BODY = '''<h2>What the CDC Says and What We Could Not Find</h2>
<p>The CDC says more than 1 in 4 older adults falls each year, that fewer than half tell their doctor, and that a single fall doubles the chance of another. A transport chair does not prevent falls, but it lets someone push you for the distances that tire you.</p>
<p>We found no federal guidance written specifically for transport chairs, so we have not claimed any. The makers' pages and listings give the numbers that matter: weight limit, seat size, folded size and brake type. Read the manual for the chair you buy, and ask a doctor or physical therapist if you are unsure whether a transport chair, which someone else pushes, or a self-propelled chair suits you.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>Lock the rear wheels before anyone sits or stands.</strong> Every chair here has rear wheel locks or brakes, and the Medline with 12-inch wheels adds hand brakes for the pusher. Apply them before a transfer, and use the safety belt if the chair has one, as the Fly-Lite listing describes.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Transport Wheelchair</h2>
<h3>Transport chair or self-propelled chair</h3>
<p>A transport chair has small wheels and push handles, and someone else pushes it. A self-propelled chair has large rear wheels with hand rims so the rider can push. All five here are transport chairs, and they cannot be pushed by the rider.</p>
<h3>Weight</h3>
<p>The chairs run from 15 pounds on the Medline Ultra Lightweight's listing to 26 pounds on the Medline with 12-inch wheels and the Drive economy. The person who lifts it into a car should try the weight first.</p>
<h3>Seat width and weight limit</h3>
<p>Four of the five have 19-inch seats and a 300-pound limit. The Drive economy has a 17-inch seat and a 250-pound limit. Measure the rider's hips and compare.</p>
<h3>Wheels</h3>
<p>Eight-inch wheels are standard on four of the five, and the Medline offers 12-inch rear wheels. Larger wheels roll over thresholds and cracked pavement more easily.</p>
<h3>Brakes</h3>
<p>All have rear wheel locks, and only the Medline with 12-inch wheels has hand brakes on the handles. Hand brakes matter on ramps and slopes.</p>
<h3>Folded size</h3>
<p>Check the folded size against your trunk. Drive's page gives 33 by 9 by 28 inches for the Fly-Lite and 23 by 9 by 30.5 inches for the economy chair without footrests.</p>

<h2>Transport Wheelchair Questions</h2>
<h3>What is a transport wheelchair?</h3>
<p>A transport wheelchair is a light folding chair with small wheels and push handles that someone else pushes. It suits short outings, appointments and travel, where it folds into a car trunk. It is not meant for the rider to propel, because the wheels are small and have no hand rims, so if you need to move yourself, ask about a self-propelled chair.</p>
<h3>Can I push myself in a transport chair?</h3>
<p>Not with a chair like these. The wheels are small and have no hand rims, so the rider cannot push, and the chair is made to be pushed from behind. A self-propelled wheelchair has large rear wheels with rims for that, and weighs more. If you will often be alone in the chair, ask a physical therapist which type fits you.</p>
<h3>How heavy is a transport chair?</h3>
<p>The weights run from 15 pounds on the Medline Ultra Lightweight's listing to 26 pounds on the Medline with 12-inch wheels and Drive's economy chair, with Drive's page giving 16.2 pounds without footrests and 18.9 with them for the Fly-Lite. If you lift it into a car every time, try the weight first, since the footrests and cushion add to it.</p>
<h3>What weight can they hold?</h3>
<p>The Medline chairs and the Drive Fly-Lite and ATC19 list 300 pounds, and Drive's page lists 250 pounds for the economy chair. The weight limit includes the rider and anything carried on the chair, so add them up. If you are near the limit, choose the chair with the higher rating, and check the seat width as well as the weight.</p>
<h3>Do I need hand brakes on a transport chair?</h3>
<p>The Medline chair with 12-inch wheels is the only one here with hand brakes on the push handles, and its listing says they allow safe and secure transfers alongside the rear-wheel locks. The others have rear wheel locks only, which hold the chair still but do not let the pusher slow it. Hand brakes are useful on ramps and slopes, so choose them if you will push on either.</p>
<h3>Will a transport chair fit in my car trunk?</h3>
<p>Drive's page gives a folded size of 33 by 9 by 28 inches for the Fly-Lite with its riggings and 23 by 9 by 30.5 inches for the economy chair without footrests, and the Medline listings say their chairs fold to fit a car trunk. Measure your trunk opening and depth and compare, and remember that the footrests come off or swing away and need a place too.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>The Medline Transport Wheelchair with 12-inch wheels is the best pick for a caregiver. It is Amazon's number one transport chair with 12,879 reviews at 4.5, it has hand brakes on the handles, and the 12-inch rear wheels handle thresholds. It weighs 26 pounds, and we could not read Medline's own page.</p>
<p>If you lift it into a car often, the Drive Fly-Lite is the lightest chair with maker-confirmed numbers, 16.2 pounds without footrests, and the Medline Ultra Lightweight is listed at 15 pounds for less money. The Drive ATC19 sits in the middle, though its two weight figures disagree.</p>
<p>For occasional use, the Drive economy chair is the cheapest and has a limited lifetime warranty, but it has a 17-inch seat, a 250-pound limit and a heavy steel frame.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The Medline Transport Wheelchair with 12-inch wheels is the best all-round transport chair, the Drive Fly-Lite the best for a light, maker-confirmed lift and the Drive economy chair the budget pick. Check the weight limit and seat width against the rider, lock the rear wheels before any transfer and read the manual for your chair. See also our guides to <a href="/best-rollators/">rollators</a> and <a href="/best-canes/">canes</a>.</p>'''

